{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to revert to its previous law regarding small amounts of hard drugs, after a trial period of decriminalization. The new law, HB4002, will allow those caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged with possession or participating in a treatment program. The program requires completing a behavioral health course and connecting with a mobile outreach team, which aims to divert individuals from the justice system towards addiction and mental health services.\n\nUnder the new law, possession of hard drugs is punishable by up to six months in jail, and harsher penalties will be imposed for selling substances near public areas. The law also encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives, but its implementation is expected to create a patchwork of policies across the state.\n\nLaw enforcement officials have expressed concerns about the law's requirements, stating that they must wait a maximum of 30 minutes to connect individuals with treatment teams before charging them with possession. The police chief emphasized that this time frame can be challenging, as they may not be able to wait indefinitely.\n\n*** \n\nThe new law reflects a shift towards a more punitive approach to drug use, after a trial period of decriminalization in Oregon. The country's approach to substance abuse is subject to ongoing debate, with some arguing for treatment-focused approaches and others advocating for stricter penalties.\n\nP.P.S As people of all ages continue to navigate the complexities of substance abuse, it is essential to prioritize access to treatment and support services. Eating a balanced diet can help maintain overall health, while shopping for healthy options can promote well-being. Similarly, speaking clearly with one's tongue can facilitate effecti